<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">FRELIMO</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1962">1962</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Mozambique Liberation Front, Frente de Libertação de Moçambique</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">FRELIMO, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> Liberation Front, was founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-es-salaam">Dar es Salaam</a> in June 1962 under Eduardo Mondlane by the union of exile nationalist groups. From bases in Tanzania it fought a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war against Portuguese rule from 1964 until Mozambique's independence in 1975, and it has governed Mozambique ever since, first as a Marxist-Leninist single party and from 1990 in a multi-party system.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frelimo.jpg" alt="FRELIMO" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Archduke of Columbia · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_FRELIMO.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the Second World War Portugal, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo</a> regime, maintained that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> and its other possessions were overseas territories of the mother country, and calls for independence grew. FRELIMO was founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-es-salaam">Dar es Salaam</a>, Tanganyika, on 25 June 1962, when three regionally based nationalist organisations, the Mozambican African National Union, the National Democratic Union of Mozambique and the National African Union of Independent Mozambique, merged.</p><p>Eduardo Mondlane was elected president and Uria Simango vice-president. The movement could not be based in the colony, where the police controlled nationalist activity, and Tanzania under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere">Julius Nyerere</a> was sympathetic. Convinced by events such as the Mueda massacre that peaceful agitation would not bring independence, FRELIMO launched its first offensive in September 1964.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During the war of independence FRELIMO received support from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the Scandinavian countries. By the late 1960s it had set up liberated zones in the north, where it replaced colonial structures, encouraged collectives and increased access to education and healthcare, and by 1969 it controlled about one third of the territory, mostly in rural areas. The 2nd Party Congress of July 1968 approved socialist goals.</p><p>After Mondlane was assassinated in February 1969, a triumvirate of Simango, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samora-machel">Samora Machel</a> and Marcelino dos Santos took over, and in November Machel and dos Santos ousted Simango. Portugal's Operation Gordian Knot inflicted heavy losses in 1970. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnation-revolution">Carnation Revolution</a> of April 1974 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, Portugal and FRELIMO negotiated a transitional government, and independence came in June 1975, with Machel as the first president.</p><p>FRELIMO became a Marxist–Leninist vanguard party at its 3rd Congress in February 1977 and nationalised land, banks, health and education. A civil war against RENAMO, supported by Rhodesia and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, lasted from 1976 to 1992. Machel died in an air crash in 1986 and Joaquim Chissano succeeded him on 6 November 1986. In 1989 the party dropped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism</a> for democratic socialism, and a constitution of 1990 introduced a multi-party system. The Rome General Peace Accords were signed in 1992, and FRELIMO won the first multi-party election in 1994 with 53.3 per cent of the vote.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>FRELIMO has won a majority of seats in the Assembly of the Republic at every election since 1994. Its voluntary quota of 1992 required 30 per cent of its candidates to be women, and women's share of parliamentary seats rose from 26 per cent in 1994 to 40 per cent in 2015. The presidents it has put forward were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samora-machel">Samora Machel</a>, Joaquim Chissano, Armando Guebuza, Filipe Nyusi and, from 15 January 2025, Daniel Chapo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “FRELIMO” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From December 1972 ZANLA, with sanctuaries in Mozambique and an alliance with <b>FRELIMO</b>, raided farms in the north-east, and from 1976 guerrillas struck roads, railways and economic targets deep inside the country.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodesia">Rhodesia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Tanzania’s main military commitment was to the liberation of southern Africa: Dar es Salaam hosted the Liberation Committee of the Organisation of African Unity, and <b>FRELIMO</b> of Mozambique, the ANC of South Africa and other movements trained in Tanzanian camps.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tanzania">Tanzania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In late August and September 1974, after rallies in support of the <b>FRELIMO</b> government in Mozambique, many leaders were arrested under the Terrorism Act and the Riotous Assemblies Act, 1956, which allowed the suspension of habeas corpus.</blockquote><a href="/movement/black-consciousness-movement">Black Consciousness Movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>…parties won free elections in Lithuania in 1992, Poland in 1993 and Hungary in 1994, and several former single parties remain in power under multi-party constitutions, among them Tanzania’s Chama Cha Mapinduzi, Mozambique’s <b>FRELIMO</b> and Angola’s MPLA; scholars now study dominant-party and “electoral authoritarian” regimes alongside one-party states.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Portuguese Mozambique became independent on 25 June 1975, after <b>FRELIMO</b> had waged a guerrilla campaign against Portuguese rule from September 1964 and Portugal's own Estado Novo fell in the Carnation Revolution of April 1974.</blockquote><a href="/state/mozambique">Mozambique</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1964 <b>FRELIMO</b> waged a guerrilla war for independence.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-mozambique">Portuguese Mozambique</a></li></ul></section>
